<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Africa Act 1909</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> Act 1909 was the act of the British Parliament that united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, Natal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a> as the Union of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, which came into being on 31 May 1910. Based on a constitution drafted by a national convention of the colonies, it placed parliament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, the executive at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and the appeal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, and largely restricted the franchise to whites outside the Cape.</p></div>
